Z||000011||firstfiero||12-29-2001||12:49 PM||||     The saleen isn't available yet and the vector never was. They always run out of money and backers befor they can go into production. I watched a show just the other week on speed vision and even though the saleen isn't into production yet I certanly think it will be very soon. <P>------------------<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by firstfiero (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackStang95||12-29-2001||01:15 PM||||"The Saleen S7 went on sale at its introduction at the famed Monterey Historic Races on August 19, 2000"<P>That was off saleens website.  Although production is going to be extremely limited (300-400 cars over 5 years) it's still available and for sale.  And a hell of a car.<P>Saleen also makes the fastest stock Mustang car ever made.  It beats vipers, 911 turbos, vettes, lambourghinis, ferraris, etc soundly in every performance catagory.  But I digress  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||12-29-2001||01:31 PM||||I guess more accurately the Fiero is "The only American mass-produced mid-engined car." Like the 80 Turbo Trans Am was the first "American made mass-produced Turbo V8 passenger car."  <P>Really, where do you draw the line?  The Saleen S7 is designed to compete against the McLaren F1 - not exactly available at your local dealership.  But, yes, it is a production car, in the sense that it's produced.<P>It gets even more muddy.  Cars like the Saleen (any of them) or the SLP Firehawks and Camaro SS's, the Grand Prix GTX, etc.  All of these cars are available through the dealership, but are modified by an aftermarked vendor.  Even the Mera falls into this catagorey.  The line between factory available and aftermarket produced gets blurrier and blurrier as manufacturers would rather contract out specialty work than do it in house.<P>You could always go back to the racing homologation rules used in the 60's.  In order for a car to be available for any "stock" or "production" class of racing, a certian number had to be built and sold to the public.  I believe that was around 2000-2500.  By that ruling, the Saleen S7 wouldn't qualify as a "production" car.  ||||reg||6
Z||000014||GTFiero1||12-29-2001||01:56 PM||||i had a post like this som time ago. The Vector was available. but there were less than 50 made. corvair was rear engined, pantera was italian just using a ford engine, the DeLorean was built in ireland. personally i dont like the saleen, i saw the same special on saleen on speed vision, it was a re-run, but to me it looked like a cheap kitcar, the body panels looked thin and they seemed to be vibrating a little going around the track. but in my thread we settled on this: the Fiero was americas only mass produced mid-engined car.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_3.jpg">   <BR>you gotta keep on keepin on...<BR>IM AOL: GTFiero &  Rumpdetective (dont ask)||||reg||1
Z||000015||Return of Fiero||12-29-2001||02:41 PM||||Wait, you mean there were other car made than the Fiero? Damn, and i thought the only car ever made was the Fiero. Whats that? Fiero is made by a larger company? ok, so it the only mid engine sports car made in america thats affordable, made by pontiac, avalible with a l4 and a v6, with out with out wing, ummmm... how specific before i hit the fiero on the head?<P>now i know we all love the Fieros, but to be honest, the only label you could put on it and be right is "The only American (canadian, like mine) made Mid-engine sports car FOR ME" you wouldnt catch me dead the that ugly ford a few posts up. i would rather drive my old lotus (i miss that car) before i even THINK about driving a car from a company that has sold me nothing but CRAPPY CARS. but thaks for the offer!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Later Gator,<BR>Myke||||reg||1
Z||000016||TRiAD||12-29-2001||02:55 PM||||* There were 17-19 Vector W8s made during it's 2 year production. <P>* There were as many as 100 vector M12s made.<P>* The S7 is officially available, but none have been delivered to non-race customers, since the race program is taking so much effort. (Selling too many S7Rs, it seems)<P>* The line is EASY. "The Fiero is the only AFFORDABLE mid-engined American made car.". <P>The Vectors, Sallen, Moslers are NOT "aftermarket upgrades" to an existing car, or some "kit". <P>BTW, I CAN buy a Saleen S7 from my local Ford dealer. <P>Some people apparently refuse to be educated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||StuGood||12-29-2001||03:31 PM||||Well, if you go back far enough, I bet you can find some other midengine American-made cars.  The L29 Cord springs to mind:  Front midengine config.(engine behind the transaxle), front wheel drive... kind of ahead of its time.  Also, didn't some of the earliest Cadillacs have the (2-cylinder) engine under the seat?  It doesn't get much more midengine than that!<P>Of course, those probably don't qualify because back then, production rates were tiny.  And the L29 Cord probably required a mortgage even back in its day.||||reg||6
Z||000018||cadero2dmax||12-29-2001||03:36 PM||||When you buy that Saleen, and pay the appropriate luxery tax, and insure the thing, be careful not to put it on the street. Wouldn't it be disasterous if the paint were to get a bird dropping on it, or a gravel chip?<P>These ain't cars, they are rich mens toys. None of the mentioned vehicles were ever meant to be on the streets of Tulsa, OK - or Morton. IL - or any other place - as a daily driver. They were meant to be toys.<P>This discussion is on a list that caters to cars that (even NEW) were never worth over $15,000. Hell, the taxes on the Saleen cost more than that!! <P>Seems like the cold weather and snows have put a lot of people inside, where they can play "what if" about Fieros on their computers!<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000019||firstfiero||12-29-2001||03:51 PM||||       I love how people just love to talk down to other people on message boards. You just know he wouldn't talk down to people like he is the god of info. if he was standing here in front of you....lol<BR>      Anyway who cares the fiero is the only mass produced mid engine car ever made in america. 100 vectors...which I didn't know they made that many. Thanks for educating me.... does not constitute mass production. Is their a dealership near by that I can go in today sign papers and drive out with it..no?...Then it's not mass produced. I'm sure long befor the vector and the fiero that their were some show cars made in the good old us of a with the engine in the middle. And lets say that they made 2 or 3 of these afermentioned cars to run the show circuit. Again that does not make it a production car.<BR>    <BR>    Even the Saleen...which I love..is not considered a production. It's to limited quanity. <P>    Again when I say the fiero is the only mid engined car produced by america I mean a car that I can drive down to my local dealership and pick one up. Not get on a waiting list to be one of the lucky few.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||TRiAD||12-29-2001||05:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>...Anyway who cares the fiero is the only mass produced mid engine car ever made in america. 100 vectors...does not constitute mass production.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, they are considered a "production" vehicle by EVERY automotive periodical, and by he Government, but YOU don't think they are, so, since you're so special, we'll just call Gerald Wiegert up and tell him his production W8s weren't "really" production because YOU don't think so.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Is their a dealership near by that I can go in today sign papers and drive out with it..no?...Then it's not mass produced.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, so the 1995 Corvette wasn't mass-produced. Can I go to a dealership and just buy one? No. They're NO LONGER in production. <P>Can I go to a dealer nearby and buy a Lamborghini?! NO. ...But they're still mass-produced. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I'm sure long befor the vector and the fiero that their were some show cars made in the good old us of a with the engine in the middle. And lets say that they made 2 or 3 of these afermentioned cars to run the show circuit. Again that does not make it a production car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When did I say it did?! When a car enters <I>series production</I>, it is a production car. The Tucker? 48 made...still a production car. Lotus 340R, 200 made, still production. <P>The Fiero is simply NOT the USs first or only production mid-engined car. You can make rules up in your head all you want, but the fact remains, you're in opposition to the people who make these determinations in your ignorance. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Even the Saleen...which I love..is not considered a production. It's to limited quanity.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, the DOT, EPA, and Saleen (as well as your local Ford dealer) disagree with you. I guess we should tell them they're wrong.  <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Again when I say the fiero is the only mid engined car produced by america I mean a car that I can drive down to my local dealership and pick one up. Not get on a waiting list to be one of the lucky few.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ah, so anything made in limited numbers is NOT production?! You better tell that to everyone with an anniversary edition ANYTHING, any of the '88 Formulas, or anything else remotely rare. Scarcity does not negate production status. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||BlackStang95||12-29-2001||06:06 PM||||Someone said fieros werent worth over 15k new...the gt's costed nearly 20k back in the mid 80's...they weren't THAT affordable.  And some think their value will continue to rise (all of you who defended coolblue).  You can't have it both ways...its either affordable or it isn't.  I'd agree with the only affordable mid engined car in america title, but you cant turn around then and say its exotic or semi-exotic or collectable like an old thunderbird.  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||BAM-BAM||12-29-2001||06:15 PM||||  Here in Vegas you can go to a dealership and buy Lambo from what they have in stock or you can order one specially taylored for you. I can even go down to Shelby and buy the new Series-1 from their production stock or special order one even saw a Diablo in a showroom.<BR>  Triad, I'm sure the argument is as diverse as the number of people involved in it. However, the Fiero is americas mid-engine flagship and no one can argue that. OK, well you can if you want.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||1
Z||000023||StuGood||12-29-2001||06:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Someone said fieros werent worth over 15k new...the gt's costed nearly 20k back in the mid 80's...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>FWIW, back when I was shopping for a (new or used) Fiero at a local Pontiac dealership in 1986, the asking price for a <B>fully</B>-equipped Fiero GT (Cruise, Fancy-dancy stereo, spoiler, gee-whiz wheels, sunroof, electric rear window de-frogger  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, etc.) was exactly $15k.  The GT's available there tended to be equipped to the hilt, BTW.  Well, alright, no curb feelers available  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>A (very) slightly used 1986 GT was for sale<BR>(&lt;1500 miles) for under $10k and I almost talked myself into buying it.  But it was an automatic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  Oh, well. ||||reg||1
