Z||000033||TRiAD||12-29-2001||10:53 PM||||OK, this is clearly going no where. <P>The facts are;<P>* There have been several US-built mid-engined <B>production</B> cars. <P>* Most of those cars are/were exotics, as it should be. <P>* The Fiero is/was the most "affordable" and was built in the highest numbers.<P>Many of you arguing these points really agree with them, except for the "production" status of the exotics. I'm sorry you feel that way, but the fact of the matter is, YOU don't make the rules, neither do I. The <B>production</B> status of the Vector W8, W12, Mosler's MT900 and the Saleen S7 was decided LONG before this thread was started. I can only suggest you open your closed minds and READ UP ON THEM. <P>I'm most likely not going to post again in this thread, it's all the same stuff over and over. If there's anything new, I may. <P>Good day. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||kenny86GT||12-29-2001||11:00 PM||||<P>Yes, most of these were/are expensive, but the people saying "America's ONLY mid-engined car" said nothing about it being the only "affordable" car. [/B][/QUOTE]<P>Saying "AMERICA'S", means saying affordable, to the average JOE. <P>------------------<BR>Those who are crazy enough to think they can change the world...DO! Kenny Starcher.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Blacktree||12-30-2001||01:31 AM||||Geez, so much arguing and posturing about something so trivial...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Here's my offering: <B>"America's only mid-engine commuter car"</B><P>HA!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000036||TRiAD||12-30-2001||04:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kenny86GT:<BR><B>Saying "AMERICA'S", means saying affordable, to the average JOE.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Gee, I thought saying "America's", as in "America's only mid-engined production car" meant <B>made in America</B>. <P>So now, doctors, lawyers, CEOs, stock market gurus...none of these people are "Americans"?! <P>Some people have an interesting explanation for everything. <P>So, what's the big deal here?! We're not bashing the Fiero, or even taking one ounce from it's appeal by recognizing that there have been a few other people who also have developed and produced mid-engined domestics. Are you people afraid that if you can't call your car "the ONLY car like it!" that it won't be worth as much?! <P>The Fiero has a special place in automotive history...on it's own merits, and in full view of the fact that it is one of SEVERAL mid-engined cars produced at one time or another in the US. <BR>It was the only one affordable to the common man,<BR>It was the only one produced in large numbers,<BR>It helped it's creators (GM) develop technologies for other lines (Saturn),<BR>It was (and is increasingly) easy to modify into something more than itself.<P>There's no reason to come up with commical, creative descriptions and interperetations of what "American" or "Production" means...that only makes you look like you're belittling the Fiero in fear that if it's not the only car like it, it's worthless. These terms have already been laid out. There's no denying them. <P>The statement "The Fiero is the only production mid-engined car made in America" is simply not true. Now, add to that statement a term like "affordable" or "made in large numbers" or "available to the masses", and you're on the money...but let's quit trying to make our cars something they simply aren't. <P>It is enough that they are what they are...let them boast on their own merits, and on the facts alone. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||kenny86GT||12-30-2001||04:31 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Gee, I thought saying "America's", as in "America's only mid-engined production car" meant [b]made in America</B>. <P>So now, doctors, lawyers, CEOs, stock market gurus...none of these people are "Americans"?! <P>Some people have an interesting explanation for everything. <P>I really had no opinion on the subject, I just wanted to be stupid... I don't care if the car "IS" or "ISN'T" the only American Mid-Engine car, I have one and that's all that counts. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Those who are crazy enough to think they can change the world...DO! Kenny Starcher.||||reg||7
Z||000038||firstfiero||12-30-2001||11:14 AM||||     Damn I'm sorry Triad I forgot that your one of the members on this board that is all knowing and NO ONE is aloud to have an opionion except you! It's my op. period. I don't consider them production cars if I can't drive to a dealership and pick one up. Mass produced in my op. doesn't mean a warehouse where the cars are being built by hand it means an assemble line. Again thats my opp.<BR> <BR>      What's was your point of starting this topic anyway other then to p### people off and start a flame war. <BR>    <BR>      If you want to believe that when someone makes 3 cars in their garage that meets all goverment standards is now a production car good for you. Your aloud to believe that,your aloud to have an op. I just personaly do not. <P><P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000039||Mickey_Moose||12-30-2001||12:16 PM||||First things...<P>quote : You could always go back to the racing homologation rules used in the 60's. In order for a car to be available for any "stock" or "production" class of racing, a certian number had to be built and sold to the public. I believe that was around 2000-2500. By that ruling, the Saleen S7 wouldn't qualify as a "production" car. <P>**this rule only applied to cars that were to enter stock car racing, there had to be so many cars of the same configuration made available to the public.<P>The Tucker &gt;&gt; they had to make 50 cars to be called a production and be eligible for entry into the market.<P>**for a car today to be called a 'production' car one would have to look at today's current guide lines.<P>Vector/etc &gt;&gt; yes they are mid engined cars and such, but you also have to consider that these are custom made cars for a certain market (ie, they are not available to the mass public...1) due to limited numbers made, 2) cost) so it's really hard to group them into the same class as the Mustang, Corvette, Firebird, Camaro, Fiero, Porche, Lada or whatever else you want to add to this group. <P>Technically any kitcar on the market based on the Fiero can be called another American made mid engine car if you want to group the Vector and others into this group.<P>BTW &gt;&gt; the Saleem, was built for racing...this is a direct comment from Saleem himself, they were never meant to be a road car.<P>I think when most people say the only North Amercain built car, they are talking about massed produced. On a side note, the Corvette is 'technically' a mid engine car when looked at in terms on weight distrubution, only the term 'behind the driver' does not place it into this class.<P>...arguing the point it mute - some people will always call it that, just like the certain individuals that keep saying the 88 suspension was desgined by Lotus  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>just my 2 cents...<P>Tim<P>||||reg||1
Z||000040||stingant0||12-30-2001||01:19 PM||||DID the Deloren have a volvo 2.8? ||||reg||1
Z||000041||Master_Sushi||12-30-2001||01:30 PM||||Wasn't the Deloren rear engine?||||reg||5
Z||000042||firstfiero||12-30-2001||04:07 PM||||    The Tucker didn't have to make 50 cars to quailify as a production car. They had to make 50 cars to retain their lease on the huge warehouse they rented from the goverment. I Watched the movie 50 times  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> For some reason I love it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000043||TRiAD||12-30-2001||04:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>    The Tucker didn't have to make 50 cars to quailify as a production car. They had to make 50 cars to retain their lease on the huge warehouse they rented from the goverment. I Watched the movie 50 times   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> For some reason I love it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly.<P>And no, I'm not the only person allowed to have an opinion, and I did not start this thread to start a flame war. <P>HOWEVER, when you're dealing with a term that already exists, and that you did not define, your opinion (and mine) is meaningless. <P>This is like saying "I think the word "at" really means "cat", since my 2 year old can't say it right. This is just my opinion, and that you think it means something else is your opinion". <P>I know you don't see what I'm saying. We didn't defing what IS and ISN'T considered a "production" car in the US, the US government did. THEY decided that Vector Aeromotive Group met the criteria fot the W8 Twin Turbo to go into series production. THEY decided that Saleen met the criteria for the S7 (not the S7R, which IS a race car) to be a production car. They decided the Mosler MT900 met their criteria to be a production car. <P>And to compare the W8 twin turbo to a KIT CAR is ludicrous. Try looking it up, see what this car is. It is simply the BEST US-built car...EVER. From an engineering standpoint, it nearly gives the Space Shuttle a run for it's money. <P>From Saleen's website; "The Saleen S7’s shape provides arguably the best overall aerodynamics of any <B>production</B> car ever built."<P>"With a mid-chassis engine location, the S7 features a trunk in the front and rear of the vehicle. <B>The S7 comes equipped with custom-fit luggage for use on long trips</B>."<P>"<B>Traditional creature comforts included on the S7 are power windows and locks, as well as air conditioning.  An AM/FM audio system with 6-disc CD player is standard</B>."<P>"Top speed, quickness, and racecar handling are combined with luxury <B>and everyday road dynamics</B>."<P>"In designing the S7, Saleen put drivability on a wide variety of road surfaces high on the list of objectives.  And, [the S7] is also a car that can be driven with pleasure on highways, autobahns and back roads."<P>Limited numbers? Yes. Less than 20 dealerships in the US? Yes. Is the S7 a <B>production car</B>? Yes. <P>I really didn't expect this kind of response. Most people here have usually appeared to be quite level-headed and intelligent. <P>The fact is WE don't define what we think is a production car, based on where (if) we can buy one, how many are made, of (in most cases here it seems) whether we've heard of it. US production car sttus is determined by the US government, like it or not. <P>It's not an argument of semantics, it's an argument of facts. Quit arguing your opinion and what you think you know, and look it up. <P>I really did not intend this thread to go this way. "I hadn't heard of that one", or "THAT was a PRODUCTION CAR?" were expected responses, not "I'M deciding that these AREN'T production cars, since I've never heard of them". <P>That's just sad. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000044||firstfiero||12-30-2001||05:25 PM||||    Of course you did...Their is no reason to get on a fiero board and say what you did and not want to start a flame war!<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000045||BC-GT||12-30-2001||06:00 PM||||Maybe you have a point, I don't really care all that much, I just wanted to note that slogan was introduced in 1984 or so and maybe it was true at the time.||||reg||1
Z||000046||Formula88||12-30-2001||06:33 PM||||And after reading all the arguing, it illustrates my point.  The Fiero is the only American <B><I>mass-produced</B></I> mid-engined car.  Yup, you can get Saleen S7's and I don't know if the Vector is still available, but you could get one when it was.  They are definately production vehicles.  They are definately <B>NOT</B> mass produced.  Can we at least agree on that?<P>The argument about a 95 Vette not being a production car because it's out of production is so stupid I won't even bother.  But I will point out how stupid it is.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>One other point people seem to have missed.  Yup, you can buy a Saleen from Ford.  Just the same as you can buy a Firehawk from Pontiac and a Camaro SS from Chevy.  The same as a Mera was available from Pontiac back in 87-88.  NONE of these cars were built by their respective manufacturers, though.  In each case, they built the base vehicle and shipped it to an aftermarket vendor, who customized the car accordingly and returned it to the dealership to be sold.  I'm not saying a Saleen is not a Ford or available at a Ford dealer.  I'm saying Ford didn't build it - at least not completely to it's "as delivered" state.<P>The C&C T-Tops in Fieros were the same way.  Even the ones delivered with T-Tops from the factory were done by C&C, not Pontiac.  Still available from Pontiac (back then), but Pontiac didn't build it like that.<P>Ok, I'm done.  You guys have fun arguing.  And don't forget to wash your hands before supper.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000047||Formula88||12-30-2001||06:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stingant0:<BR><B>DID the Deloren have a volvo 2.8? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It was the PRV (Pugeot/Renault/Volvo) 2.8 V6, but I always heard it referred to as a Renault V6.||||reg||1
Z||000048||CoolBlue87GT||12-30-2001||06:55 PM||||W h a t e v e r.<P>Aruge this, The Fiero is the best and only mid engine, 2 seater production car made by Pontiac so far.<P>D o n e.<P>Okay Harry Potter ?||||reg||1
Z||000049||TRiAD||12-30-2001||08:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>And after reading all the arguing, it illustrates my point.  The Fiero is the only American [b]<I>mass-produced</B></I> mid-engined car.  Yup, you can get Saleen S7's and I don't know if the Vector is still available, but you could get one when it was.  They are definately production vehicles.  They are definately <B>NOT</B> mass produced.  Can we at least agree on that?...[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ABSOLUTELY. I tried to conceide that point from the first post. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>One other point people seem to have missed. Yup, you can buy a Saleen from Ford. Just the same as you can buy a Firehawk from Pontiac and a Camaro SS from Chevy. The same as a Mera was available from Pontiac back in 87-88. NONE of these cars were built by their respective manufacturers, though. In each case, they built the base vehicle and shipped it to an aftermarket vendor, who customized the car accordingly and returned it to the dealership to be sold. I'm not saying a Saleen is not a Ford or available at a Ford dealer. I'm saying Ford didn't build it - at least not completely to it's "as delivered" state.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Never said the Saleen was a Ford, which it of course is not. I said it is a production vehicle, which it is. It's hardly comparable to the Mera kitcar strangely available from Pontiac dealers, or the SVT/SLP cars. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FirstFiero:<BR><B>Of course you did...Their is no reason to get on a fiero board and say what you did and not want to start a flame war!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What, because you disagree? Whatever. It was never intended to become a flame war. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B>Aruge this, The Fiero is the best and only mid engine, 2 seater production car made by Pontiac so far.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nothing to argue there, man. <P>Sounds like we may be making progress?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000050||Cooter||12-30-2001||10:01 PM||||According to <A HREF="http://www.vectoraeromotiveclub.com/archive/news/failurestory/failurepage.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.vectoraeromotiveclub.com/archive/news/failurestory/failurepage.asp</A>  , the company that made the Vector was started in 1987. So, the Fiero was ahead by a few years in their slogan.<P>According to  <A HREF="http://www.moslerauto.com/mt900web/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.moslerauto.com/mt900web/index.htm</A>  , mosler did not build a car until 1985, a year after the Fiero's slogan.<P>Saleen is currently building their supercars, 17 years after the Fiero.<P>I don't understand your point because at the time of initial production, their slogan was somewhat accurate. Since the introduction of our cars, there have been others make an automobile of similiar layout. Should we dispose of all our old Pontaic sales literature now because it does not apply any longer? How about Buick's add campaign that said "The fastest production car built in America" from the GNX Regal? Since that is no longer true, should we crucify anyone who quotes that historically accurate slogan too?||||reg||1
Z||000051||Slammed Fiero||12-30-2001||10:38 PM||||I haven't read this entire thread , nor do I plan too.<P>But I see the Saleen S7 Mentioned...<P>Since when does the installation of a motor constitute "American made"<P>The entire chassis and body of the car is constructed in England. I had a tour of the Saleen shop last year courtesy of a road racer friend. Saleen puts the motor in , the A-Arms on , hooks it all up ..ect..ect. They get the cars in crates from England.<P>It's like buying a kit car basically.<P>Another comment on the Fiero being nearly 20 Grand New. I have the sales sheet from my car. The original owner (I'm the 2nd) Signed the deal at 16,984 Canadian in 1986 , Fully loaded.<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000052||BN Boomer||12-30-2001||11:06 PM||||Let's see here. You claim it was never intended to become a flame war but you have to come back and argue every point made, no matter how mundane or insignificant. If YOU don't like the slogan, then don't use it. It doesn't mean it's not a fitting description to the rest of us. Why do you have such a burr in your panties about it anyhow? Try being a little less anal. Life might actually be enjoyable.||||reg||13
Z||000053||BlackStang95||12-31-2001||12:42 AM||||Jm 17 thousand after incentives and talk-down is almost 20k original price.  Thanks for proving my point for me.||||reg||1
Z||000054||TRiAD||12-31-2001||02:51 AM||||Cooter, yeah, I know it came before the others, I was referring to people who use that statement now, like the Fiero is still the only MR car ever made in the US. Sorry for the confusion. <P>Slammed, you're right on a lot of the Saleen info, the car is mostly assembled in England, but final assembly is done here, and really, a strong case could be made that MANY "domestics" aren't, being fully assembled in Mexico and/or Canada (S10 truck, most of the F bodies, etc) I wasn't going that far with my distinctions. I was going by final assembly. Again, sorry for the confusion. <P>Boomer; "It doesn't mean it's not a fitting description to the rest of us." Um, if it's wrong information, yes it does. <P>What it comes down to is this, the main place I was referring to people using this where it bothered me is in public sale of their Fiero. If you're selling a $14K '88GT with no miles on it, looking for collectors, and you bill the car as "the only US mid-engined car", you're misleading potential buyers. This could potentially get you into legal trouble, and you could end up having to buy a car back, because of an unfortunate lack of good information. <P>I hope that clears this up a bit. <P>We're all here to share knowledge relative to these cars, and I thought I would share some. It is corrective info, and that apparently burns some people's egos. It wasn't intended to. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000055||soup||12-31-2001||04:01 AM||||I can't beleive how unbelievably pathetic and yet funny this is.<P>I know I am going to get flammed for this, but seriously, learn to respect the opinions of others.<P>I am only 17 years old, and in my somewhat limited experience of life, I am beginning to notice how unbelievably stupid, purposely ignorant, and down right pathetic people are! <P>When Triad says that he started this not as a flame war, and some ass comes back saying "then why start it?", how arrogant are we? Yes and I know I am being hypocritical but it is hard not to be. <P>From what I read, Triad is trying to state a point, and he has had good points, now others have rebutted quite respectably but others are complete morons. When he's stating facts, and his points are good, and true. Take your head out of your asses and read what he is saying! You will find it makes sense. It did take a bit of clearing up but seriously! The fact that during a sale this could become a lawsuit, and with the state of America's judicial system (as well as ours up here in Canada) this is a valid point, and it was just a beef in the first place. Now I know im not omnipotent, but istead of flamming, use your head, add some intelligence to your conversations, and respect others decisions. <P>And again i know I'm being hypocritical, but its over a computer, and you still have to shove your own pride in everyone's face by arguing over miniscule things!<P>So yeah.. go ahead and flame me! Maybe its the shitty day I had, maybe I read everything out of context.. who knows.<P>------------------<BR>87 GT with T-Tops, I wish they were from the dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000056||Formula88||12-31-2001||09:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Jm 17 thousand after incentives and talk-down is almost 20k original price.  Thanks for proving my point for me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't forget, he said $17K <B>Canadian</B> which is quite a bit lower than US$20K.  He also mentions no discounts, etc.  So your gratitude for his proving your point seems a bit premature.<P>If we're talking Lira or Yen, the number gets even higher, but the US price is still under $20K.||||reg||1
